1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,400
Why Domain Matters, part 3: Payments. The record says payment failed; the customer’s account was debited.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:10,800
The same synthetic world: Store 014 in Jayanagar, now with every one of its 30,077 June bills paid at the tills, and an answer key for every rupee.

3
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:17,700
Five readings: approvals at 95.9%, the data tests pass, 17 credits wait in suspense, and the assistant says nobody paid twice.

4
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:25,100
One basket in every record that holds it. The till says the UPI payment failed; the provider’s report says success, 87 seconds later.

5
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:31,500
In the 18:00 and 19:00 hours the bank credited 17 UPI payments that the tills had marked failed.

6
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:38,400
10 data tests pass. Five tests about how UPI works fail: 48 timeouts have no reference number to join on.

7
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:45,800
Treat a timeout as a question. Matched by amount and time: 26 credited late, 6 returned, 16 never debited. Every one right.

8
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:52,200
The demo: change what the data team does with June’s records, or rerun June with the till and the back office working differently.

9
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:59,600
The suspense account becomes a list of customers: 26 extra charges on 24, found a median 0.6 days after the debit.

10
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:07,000
With a domain layer the assistant answers 7 of 7: refunds took 4.9 days from the debit, not 3 from the complaint.

11
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:13,900
Payment success four ways: 61% at the till, 82% at the bank, 100% of bills paid, 87% of customers charged once.

12
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:20,300
Wait for the status check at 90 seconds: double charges in June fall from 24 to 9, and the tills spend 99 minutes taking payment, not 77.

13
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:26,700
Put the bill ID on every UPI request: double charges still happen, but the median refund falls from 4.9 days to 0.7.

14
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:33,100
The technical companion: the tests in SQL, the matcher, the extra charges scored against the answer key, and a notebook on the records.

15
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:39,500
For data and AI leaders: five questions to ask your team.

16
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Every record was right about what it saw. Only the customer saw both debits.
